# Copy to .env and fill in.
# Cornell LiteLLM gateway. The Anthropic SDK reads both of these from the
# environment, and the gateway serves Gemini over the Anthropic /v1/messages
# format — so the "ANTHROPIC_" names carry a LiteLLM key and a Gemini model.
ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://api.ai.it.cornell.edu
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# --- Voice ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Speech-to-speech runs through the same gateway, so there are no separate
# STT/TTS keys. REALTIME_MODEL alone decides which of the two realtime families
# the study talks to, and they do not behave alike — what each one needs is
# written down once, in REALTIME_FAMILIES in server/voice/realtime.py. Read
# that table before changing this line; it is the difference between a session
# that steers, transcribes and takes turns, and one that quietly does none of
# the three.
#
# What the gateway served on 2026-09-10 (GET /v1/models):
#   Gemini family  nto.gemini-live-2.5-flash               <- what the study runs
#                  nto.gemini-live-2.5-flash-native-audio
#   gpt family     gpt-realtime-2  gpt-realtime-2.1  gpt-realtime-2.1-mini
REALTIME_MODEL=nto.gemini-live-2.5-flash

# The voice a session opens with when nothing has cast it. Per-character
# casting is not here: it lives in scenarios/v3/*.yaml as `realtime_voice`.
# Voice names are two separate catalogues, one per family, and no name is in
# both:
#
#   Gemini Live   Puck  Charon  Kore  Fenrir  Aoede  Leda  Orus  Zephyr
#   gpt-realtime  alloy  ash  ballad  coral  echo  sage  shimmer  verse
#                 marin  cedar
#
# REALTIME_VOICE and REALTIME_MODEL MUST name the same family. Crossing them is
# the mistake that costs an actor its persona, and neither family says so in
# any way an operator would notice. Probed live on 2026-09-10, one short socket
# per case:
#
#   Gemini name, gpt model   an invalid_value error listing the ten names it
#                            does accept, and then the WHOLE session.update is
#                            discarded — the instructions with it. The actor
#                            answers fluently, as the gateway's stock
#                            assistant, in no character at all.
#   gpt name, Gemini model   accepted without a murmur. session.updated comes
#                            back, audio plays, and the voice is simply the
#                            default: `alloy` on nto.gemini-live-2.5-flash
#                            measured ~102 Hz, which is Puck. Cast a group room
#                            that way and three characters walk in as one
#                            person wearing three name badges.
#   a name in neither        the socket closes (1006) straight after
#                            session.created, with no error frame at all.
#
# server/voice/realtime.py now refuses an off-roster voice before it opens the
# socket, so the first two fail loudly rather than convincingly. That is a
# backstop, not a licence to pair them wrongly: it fails at the top of an
# encounter, with a participant already sitting there.
REALTIME_VOICE=Puck

# Moving REALTIME_MODEL to the gpt family is NOT only a config change.
# config/consent.yaml tells participants, in as many words, that their
# microphone audio is transmitted live to a speech model "operated by Google
# (Gemini)". A gpt-realtime model sends that audio to OpenAI instead, which
# makes the consent form a false statement to a person about where their voice
# went. The consent text has to change with the model, and that is an IRB
# decision rather than a deployment one. The scenario bank would move too: the
# casts in scenarios/v3/*.yaml are Gemini names, and each character's line
# names the gpt voice that stands in for it.

# The language every realtime session is told the conversation is in. It rides
# on whatever input transcription the model's family row already asks for
# (whisper-1 plus a language on the gpt route; a language-only hint on both
# Gemini routes), the actor is told to speak it whatever it thinks it heard, and
# the scribe's brief names it. origin/main cabc1dd, and it is not cosmetic: two
# live runs with an English-speaking participant produced a Russian word and a
# run of Japanese syllables in the transcript before it.
#
# Set it BLANK to send no hint at all, which is the pre-cabc1dd behaviour.
# Verified 2026-09-08 that a language-only hint mutes neither Gemini route.
TRANSCRIPTION_LANG=en

# WHICH FORM OF EACH CONSTRUCT A RUN SERVES. One of the two study designs this
# merge holds at once, and the one the code ships as the default:
#
#   A        (the default) Phase 1 pins S1A/S2A/S3A/S4A on every run, with the
#            CONSTRUCT ORDER counterbalanced per participant. origin/main
#            cabc1dd. Note what it does to the exclusion table: a pinned form
#            takes _apply_form_exclusions' "pinned by the caller" hatch, so the
#            rule that bars S1A from any run containing teamwork is OFF for the
#            whole of Phase 1 and every such run is stamped "exclusion not
#            applied". That is deliberate, and it is the PI's call.
#   B        pins the other form the same way.
#   random   the per-slot draw: twelve forms, two of each construct's three in
#            play and the third held in reserve, FORM_EXCLUSIONS applied to the
#            completed draw.
#
# A variant passed explicitly (an entry link, a second attempt) still wins over
# this, whatever it is set to. See docs/OPERATIONS.md, "Which scenarios a
# participant gets", and tests/test_default_run_variant.py.
DEFAULT_RUN_VARIANT=A

# How long the runner lets the bridge start a reply of its own before asking for
# one. NORMALLY LEAVE THIS UNSET: unset, each family uses its own measured value
# (autofire_wait in REALTIME_FAMILIES -- 1.5 s on plain flash and the gpt route,
# 4.5 s on native-audio, which fires ~3.3 s after silence). Setting it pins ONE
# number for every route, which is what this was before the families were a
# table, and on the wrong route it means two replies, both spoken and both
# transcribed.
# AUTOFIRE_WAIT=

# Default model — overridable per scenario via scenarios/*.yaml
CLAUDE_MODEL=nto.gemini-3.1-flash-lite


# Server
HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=8765

# Public hostnames (Cornell-delegated Route 53 zone). Defaults in server/app.py
# match production; override for staging or a different environment.
APP_HOST=rf.ai-ready-workforce.ai.cornell.edu
API_HOST=api.rf.ai-ready-workforce.ai.cornell.edu
# Comma-separated. ALLOWED_HOSTS guards the Host header; empty disables it.
# ALLOWED_HOSTS=
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Auth — set to a random string and pass via ?key=... in the URL.
# Leave blank for local dev.
SESSION_KEY=

# Where participants are sent after finishing all four encounters
# (the Qualtrics continuation link; the run id and completion code are appended)
SURVEY_RETURN_URL=
SURVEY_RETURN_LABEL=Return to the survey

# Qualtrics API (survey response export + run joining; see server/qualtrics.py)
QUALTRICS_API_TOKEN=
QUALTRICS_SURVEY_ID=
QUALTRICS_BASE_URL=https://yul1.qualtrics.com

# --- Upstream consent ----------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE VARIABLE THAT SILENTLY VOIDS A WHOLE WAVE. Consent is taken in
# Qualtrics now, before anybody reaches /start, so this platform never sees the
# text the participant agreed to and cannot work out which version it was.
# server/storage.py therefore refuses to record ANY study consent until this
# names it: a record that says "they consented" without naming the document is a
# statement no audit can check, and the one field an IRB reads would be a guess.
#
# Unset, the failure is total and it is silent. Measured on this tree: every
# POST /api/consent answers 404, every participant record stays
# consent_given:false, every voice socket closes 4403, /health keeps answering
# 200, and /start keeps minting runs. The wave records ZERO encounters,
# uniformly, from the first arrival onward, and the first evidence of it is an
# empty dataset. There is no partial failure mode and no participant who gets
# through — so check it before the first arrival, not after the tenth.
#
# WHAT TO PUT HERE: the version string of the consent block the Qualtrics survey
# is showing right now — whatever your survey's own consent question is
# versioned as. NOT config/consent.yaml's `version`: that names the text this
# platform used to show, and stamping it on somebody who never saw it names the
# wrong document as the one they agreed to. One worked example, in the shape
# this study uses:
#
#     UPSTREAM_CONSENT_VERSION=cornell-irb-2026-09-v3
#
# It is left BLANK below on purpose. A plausible-looking version shipped in this
# file would be copied into a real deployment and stamped on real participants'
# records as the document they agreed to, which is worse than the refusal it
# would silence. Placeholders are refused for the same reason — "[FILL IN: ...]",
# TBD, TODO, changeme and the like are treated as unset (is_placeholder_value in
# server/storage.py), so a half-filled config fails closed rather than filing
# that string as a consent version.
#
# Bump it whenever the survey's consent wording is re-approved and restart the
# process; it is read per call, so nothing else needs rebuilding. On AWS this is
# set by the task definition from the `upstream_consent_version` Terraform
# variable, which has no default — see infra/terraform/ecs.tf.
UPSTREAM_CONSENT_VERSION=

# --- Webcam video storage (S3) -------------------------------------------
# The participant's webcam recording is the artefact Phase 2 rates, and these
# five names are the whole of what decides where it lands. They are read by TWO
# different mechanisms whose precedence runs in OPPOSITE directions, which is
# what makes a wrong guess here silent rather than loud:
#
#   S3_BUCKET               server/video.py reads these through
#   AWS_REGION              server.llm.setting(), where THIS FILE WINS over the
#                           ambient environment.
#
#   AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID       boto3 reads these itself, out of the PROCESS
#   AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY   environment, where THE AMBIENT ENVIRONMENT WINS
#   AWS_SESSION_TOKEN       over this file.
#
# The bridge between the two is one line: server/app.py calls load_dotenv() at
# import, which copies this file into os.environ WITHOUT overriding anything
# already there. Two consequences, both measured rather than assumed:
#
#   * A key exported in the shell beats a key written here. If S3 is reaching
#     the wrong account, look at the shell before you look at this file.
#   * A key written here reaches boto3 ONLY in a process that imported
#     server.app. An offline tool that imports server.video or
#     server.verify_record on its own never runs that line, resolves no
#     credentials, and reports every encounter as having no recording while the
#     objects sit in the bucket. For anything that is not the server, export the
#     credentials in the shell or use ~/.aws — see docs/DEPLOY-AWS.md.
#
# WHERE THIS FILE IS READ FROM IS NOT WHERE YOU ARE STANDING. python-dotenv
# walks up from server/llm.py, so the .env that counts is always the one at the
# REPOSITORY ROOT; a .env beside the terminal's working directory is not read at
# all. Measured from a working directory outside the repository, the
# repository's own .env was still the one loaded.
#
# AWS_DEFAULT_REGION IS NOT READ. server/video.py takes its region from
# AWS_REGION and passes it to every client explicitly, so the AWS CLI's
# preferred spelling moves nothing here. Measured: with AWS_DEFAULT_REGION set
# and AWS_REGION unset, video.REGION stayed us-east-1 — right until the day
# the bucket is somewhere else, when every call goes to the wrong region and
# comes back as a PermanentRedirect that names neither variable.
#
# ON AWS, LEAVE ALL FIVE UNSET. infra/terraform/ecs.tf sets S3_BUCKET and
# AWS_REGION in the task definition, and the task role supplies the credentials
# through the container credentials endpoint — there is nothing to paste and a
# long-lived key pasted into a task would outlive the role that replaced it.
# This file cannot reach a task in any case: .dockerignore is an allowlist and
# .env is not on it, so the image contains no .env at all and the task
# definition's values are the only ones there are.
S3_BUCKET=relational-fluency-study-data
AWS_REGION=us-east-1
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# Only for temporary credentials — `aws sso login`, `aws sts assume-role`, an
# AWS account that requires MFA. Leave blank for a long-lived IAM user key.
AWS_SESSION_TOKEN=

# HALF A CREDENTIAL IS WORSE THAN NONE, and it is the likeliest paste error, so
# it is worth knowing exactly what it does. Both blank is a clean "no
# credentials": botocore ignores empty strings, the credential chain resolves
# nothing, and server/video.py takes its known-no-credentials shortcut — one
# 2.3-second chain walk a minute rather than one per encounter. An ID with no
# secret is NOT that. botocore raises PartialCredentialsError out of client
# construction, the shortcut declines to guess and every S3 call makes the
# attempt and logs it, and the boot preflight reports
# `PartialCredentialsError`, naming the missing variable in its `detail`. The
# recording still survives — the presign route answers 503 and the browser
# falls back to PUTting the bytes to this server — but the console fills with
# warnings naming a variable nobody has looked at. Fill both, or neither.
#
# WHETHER IT WORKED is not a thing to find out during a wave. The boot log says
# so, and so does GET /health (the `storage` block: `ok`, `credentials`,
# `readable`, `writable`, and an `error_code` when one of them is false) — see
# docs/DEPLOY-AWS.md#webcam-recordings-and-the-study-bucket for the four states
# and what each one costs. A credential that appears AFTER boot is picked up
# without a restart: server/video.py re-walks the chain every 60 seconds, so
# `aws sso login` in the next terminal starts working inside a minute.

# How large a recording this server will accept on the local fallback route,
# PUT /api/sessions/{id}/video. Bytes, counted as they arrive rather than taken
# from Content-Length, which is a claim by the sender. Unset is 512 MiB, which
# is the default in server/app.py: a ceiling no honest recording reaches and a
# disk-filling one passes in seconds. This bounds only the FALLBACK — a
# browser-direct PUT to S3 never passes through this process, and is bounded by
# the bucket rather than by anything here.
# MAX_VIDEO_UPLOAD_BYTES=
